WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.300
At the end of the day, trust is the most important thing in extension.

2
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:20.000
Extension was effective in the United States because of the quality of the extension agents that were hired and the commitment they had to serving the community and improving the quality of life of people.

3
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.280
And without trust you’re dead in the water.

4
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:29.100
The trust credibility is the value of extension.

5
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:34.180
And that takes . . . that took the 100 years to do.

6
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:43.200
And it’s a jewel to this country; particularly when a hurricane hits.

7
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.860
And everybody’s thankful that when a disaster hits that we have an extension service.

8
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:58.330
With the disciplines we have in extension and on this campus with our Land Grant and Sea Grant mission it was a clear, established,

9
00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:06.270
obvious link to me that we could be quickly part of this solution for the state, of the biggest disaster we’ve ever faced [Hurricane Katrina].

10
00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.730
And we began working on that.

11
00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:13.730
We had to develop a volunteer management system for New Orleans.

12
00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.450
People were coming in from all over the country.

13
00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:23.230
And our Sea Grant and extension and Land Grant agents had to manage our volunteers and deploy them where they could do the most good.

14
00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:27.370
A lot of students came from other universities.

15
00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:37.610
The governor’s office, Department of Agriculture, Wildlife and Fisheries; we coordinated our work through them.

16
00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:47.530
You know, it was something as basic as “Can I plant a crop where salt water has been?” to “How do I clean this black mold in my house?”

17
00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.770
“Can I drink the water out of my water well?”

18
00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:54.290
Just simple things that have to be answered that we can immediately deploy.

19
00:01:54.290 --> 00:02:02.690
Now one thing that we learned though: the internet don’t work in a disaster, cell phones don’t work during a disaster.

20
00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:06.490
So we understood that and we had to go back to the basics.

21
00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:09.710
We had to print information and deliver it to the people.
